course: dictionary
lesson: 6904
title: What ownership means
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ownership is the fact of owning something: having it as yours by right, like a house, a dog or a bike. «Receiving a gift usually makes it yours.» When someone gives you a bike, you gain ownership of it.
explain: Ownership is a human agreement, not a law of nature like gravity. «The cat sleeping on Dad's chair has no idea it's "his".» People agree who owns what, but animals don't know these rules.
explain: You can also take ownership of a task. This means you accept it as your own responsibility and do it well, such as when a pupil takes ownership of their learning.
fact: Ownership means owning something, having it as yours by right.
fact: «Ownership is a human idea that works because people agree on it.»
fact: Ownership is not a law of nature like gravity.
fact: To take ownership of a task means to accept it as your own responsibility.
fact: Phrases you may meet: home ownership, proof of ownership, change of ownership, take ownership of.
exercise: What does ownership mean? | Losing something you once had | *Having something as yours by right | Breaking something by accident | Borrowing something for a day
exercise: Which phrase means accepting a task as your own responsibility? | *Take ownership of | Change of ownership | Proof of ownership | Home ownership
exercise: Why does the cat not know the chair is Dad's? | *Ownership is a human agreement, and animals don't know these rules | The chair is too big | Ownership is a law of nature like gravity | Cats cannot see chairs
test: «Ownership is a human agreement, not a law of nature like gravity.» Is ownership like gravity? | *No, it is a human agreement | Yes, cats follow it | Yes, it works the same for everyone | No, because it is a law of nature
test: A teacher says, «Sam has taken ownership of his reading.» What does this mean? | Sam owns a library | Sam has lost his reading book | Sam is reading in the library | *Sam accepts reading as his own responsibility and does it well
exam: Ana says ownership is the same as a law of nature, like gravity. Is she right? | *No, ownership is a human agreement that people agree on | Yes, because everyone must obey gravity and ownership | Yes, because cats know who owns the chair | No, because ownership only means lending things
